-- title:
--   Counting attempted transitions preserves the full restart result
-- statement:
--   Let $n,r\in\mathbb N$ and let $o_1,o_2$ be arbitrary Boolean functions on subset queries of $E_n=\{0,\ldots,n-1\}$. A paired state is a subset of $E_n\times\{0,1\}$, and a restart record consists of such a state, a natural bit cursor, and a natural attempt count. Let $s$ be an arbitrary annealing schedule, with cooling parameter $\rho\in\mathbb Q$, phase count $L$, transitions per stage $\tau$, shared restart-attempt cap $R$, and rejection-trial cap $T$. Let $W_a(i,k)\in\mathbb Q$ be arbitrary stored weights, indexed by $a\in\mathbb N$ and distinct $i,k\in E_n$. Fix an infinite Boolean tape and natural numbers $j,c_0$. A restart from cursor $c_0$ first draws a transversal using $n$ tape bits, then processes stages $a=1,\ldots,j$ at parameter $\rho^a$ and weights $W_a$, making $\tau$ trace-return transitions per stage under the shared attempt cap. The counted version retains an auxiliary number of actual chain-step calls even if the public result aborts. Then $$\operatorname{value}(\operatorname{countedRestart}(j,c_0))_1=\operatorname{value}(\operatorname{restartPhase}(j,c_0)).$$ Both computations use the same $r,o_1,o_2,s,W$ and tape, and the equality includes the absent result on abort.
-- source:
--   https://github.com/meelgroup/counting-matroid/blob/dd16a4628fdb095c58fe0f3dfd8e353c6e97443c/CountingMatroid/Analysis/RestartAttemptAccounting.lean#L223-L266

